`renderThumb` is an internal render helper defined in the component body that builds its own nested JSX tree (`Tooltip > PressableWithFeedback > Text`) inline and closes over parent state via the `rate` handler (which drives `setHasThanked`). Internal `render*` helpers that produce non-trivial nested JSX hide the real render tree from the return statement and are exactly what this rule targets — the fix is to extract the helper into its own named component with explicit props rather than keeping it as an in-body closure.

Extract a dedicated component, e.g.:

```tsx
type ConciergeFeedbackThumbProps = {
    /** The emoji to react with */
    emoji: Emoji;

    /** Accessibility label and tooltip text */
    label: string;

    /** Called when the thumb is pressed */
    onPress: () => void;
};

function ConciergeFeedbackThumb({emoji, label, onPress}: ConciergeFeedbackThumbProps) {
    const styles = useThemeStyles();
    return (
        <Tooltip text={label}>
            <PressableWithFeedback
                style={[styles.conciergeFeedbackThumb, styles.userSelectNone]}
                hoverStyle={styles.conciergeFeedbackThumbHovered}
                pressStyle={styles.conciergeFeedbackThumbHovered}
                onPress={onPress}
                accessibilityLabel={label}
                role={CONST.ROLE.BUTTON}
                pressDimmingValue={1}
                dataSet={{[CONST.SELECTION_SCRAPER_HIDDEN_ELEMENT]: true}}
                sentryLabel={CONST.SENTRY_LABEL.CONCIERGE_FEEDBACK.THUMB}
            >
                <Text style={styles.conciergeFeedbackThumbEmoji}>{emoji.code}</Text>
            </PressableWithFeedback>
        </Tooltip>
    );
}
```

Then render `<ConciergeFeedbackThumb ... />` in the return instead of calling `renderThumb(...)`.

`renderChildren` is an internal render helper that does real work — it iterates `React.Children.toArray(children)`, groups the consecutive `TableHeader`/`TableBody` run, and builds new `role="table"` `<View>` wrappers inline — hiding the actual render tree from the component's `return`. Internal functions that return JSX and carry non-trivial grouping/wrapping logic should live in their own focused, independently testable unit rather than as a closure in the component body.

Extract this into a small dedicated component (e.g. `TableSemanticContainer`) that receives `children`, `isEnabled`, `title`, `rowCount`, and `columnCount` as explicit props and performs the header/body grouping itself, then render it directly in the JSX:

```tsx
<TableContext.Provider value={...}>
    <TableSemanticContainer
        isEnabled={isTableSemanticsEnabled}
        title={title}
        rowCount={processedData.length}
        columnCount={semanticColumnCount}
    >
        {children}
    </TableSemanticContainer>
    <Modal ... />
</TableContext.Provider>
```

This keeps the render tree visible in `return`, isolates the grouping logic, and lets React Compiler memoize the extracted component.

This single anonymous `useEffect` bundles several distinct responsibilities inline in the component body: (1) gating on collection load status, (2) recording the set of pre-existing agents into a mutable ref, (3) detecting creation failure and navigating back, and (4) scanning the whole collection to identify the newly-created agent and opening its DM. That is a lot of non-trivial matching/business logic living directly in the render function, coupled to the `pendingCreatedAgentRef` mutation and the Onyx collection shape. Per this rule, even a single effect benefits from extraction into a focused, well-named, independently testable hook.

Extract this into a dedicated hook (e.g. `useOpenDMWithCreatedAgent`) that owns the concern end-to-end, so `AddAgentPage` just calls it:

```tsx
// useOpenDMWithCreatedAgent.ts — owns the "wait for the created agent, then open its DM" concern
function useOpenDMWithCreatedAgent() {
    const chatWithAgent = useChatWithAgent();
    const [agentPrompts, agentPromptsMetadata] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.SHARED_NVP_AGENT_PROMPT);
    const pendingCreatedAgentRef = useRef<PendingCreatedAgent | null>(null);

    useEffect(() => {
        // ...matching + navigation logic moved here...
    }, [agentPrompts, agentPromptsMetadata.status, chatWithAgent]);

    return (optimisticAccountID: number) => {
        pendingCreatedAgentRef.current = {optimisticAccountID, knownAgentAccountIDs: null};
    };
}

// AddAgentPage.tsx
const watchForCreatedAgent = useOpenDMWithCreatedAgent();
// ...in handleSubmit, online branch:
watchForCreatedAgent(optimisticAccountID);
setIsCreatingAgent(true);
```

This keeps the matching logic isolated, named, documented, and unit-testable on its own, and slims `AddAgentPage` down to orchestration.

This adds a fourth inline `useEffect` to `DelegateAccessHandler`, whose stated purpose (per its JSDoc) is to isolate delegate-access Onyx subscriptions. This new effect is non-trivial recovery logic for a different concern — it inspects the persisted request queue, schedules a timeout, and re-fires `openApp()` to heal a stranded `IS_LOADING_APP` flag. It is also nearly identical in shape and dependency array (`[hasLoadedApp, isLoadingApp, isOffline, sessionAccountID, isLoadingAppMetadata]`) to the existing "missing isLoadingApp" recovery effect right above it, so the two recovery paths for the same Onyx key now live as two separate inline effects. Per the rule, inline `useEffect` calls are a code smell and effects serving the same purpose should be grouped into a focused, named hook.

Extract the `isLoadingApp` recovery logic into a dedicated hook that owns both the `undefined` and stranded-`true` cases, e.g.:

```tsx
// useRecoverStrandedIsLoadingApp.ts
function useRecoverStrandedIsLoadingApp() {
    const hasHandledMissingRef = useRef(false);
    const hasHandledStrandedRef = useRef(false);
    const [hasLoadedApp] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.HAS_LOADED_APP);
    const [isLoadingApp, isLoadingAppMetadata] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.IS_LOADING_APP);
    const [sessionAccountID] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.SESSION, {selector: accountIDSelector});
    const {isOffline} = useNetwork();

    // missing-flag recovery + stranded-true recovery live here, isolated and unit-testable
}

// DelegateAccessHandler.tsx
function DelegateAccessHandler() {
    useRecoverStrandedIsLoadingApp();
    // ...delegate-access effects only
}
```

This keeps `DelegateAccessHandler` focused on delegate access, gives the recovery logic a descriptive name and isolated tests, and lets both recovery cases evolve together.

This PR adds two more inline `useEffect` hooks to `ReportActionsListContent`, an already large component that aggregates many responsibilities. The new effects implement a single, self-contained concern — "follow the action-badge target and scroll to it once resolved" — with non-trivial logic (a latest-callback ref sync, directional/topmost guards, animation-delay computation, and `setTimeout`/`requestAnimationFrame` scheduling with cleanup). Inline `useEffect` calls handling a distinct concern are a code smell; they should live in a focused, named hook so the logic is isolated, self-documenting, and independently testable.

Extract this into a dedicated hook, e.g. `useFollowActionBadgeTarget`, co-located in the same directory:

```tsx
// useFollowActionBadgeTarget.ts
function useFollowActionBadgeTarget({
    isProduction,
    reportID,
    actionTargetReportActionID,
    actionBadgeTargetIndex,
    actionBadge,
    renderedVisibleReportActions,
    scrollToActionBadgeTarget,
}: UseFollowActionBadgeTargetParams) {
    const prevActionTargetReportActionID = usePrevious(actionTargetReportActionID);
    const prevActionBadge = usePrevious(actionBadge);
    const scrollToActionBadgeTargetRef = useRef(scrollToActionBadgeTarget);
    useEffect(() => {
        scrollToActionBadgeTargetRef.current = scrollToActionBadgeTarget;
    });
    useEffect(() => {
        // ...guards + scheduling...
        // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
    }, [actionTargetReportActionID]);
}

// ReportActionsList.tsx
useFollowActionBadgeTarget({isProduction, reportID, actionTargetReportActionID, actionBadgeTargetIndex, actionBadge: reportAttributes?.actionBadge, renderedVisibleReportActions, scrollToActionBadgeTarget});
```

This keeps `ReportActionsListContent` as an orchestration layer and moves the auto-scroll concern behind a well-named boundary.

`renderSubtitle` is an internal helper that builds a deeply nested JSX tree inline (nested `View`s, two `Text` nodes, a conditional `{!!ruleBotAccountID && ...}` block, and a `UserPill`), hiding the real render tree from the `return` statement. Internal functions that return non-trivial nested JSX should be extracted into their own focused component with explicit props.

Extract this into a dedicated component (e.g. `AgentRulesSubtitle`) that receives `ruleBotAccountID`, `ruleBot`, and `ruleBotDisplayName` (or fetches them itself), and render it directly in the JSX tree:

```tsx
function AgentRulesSubtitle({ruleBotAccountID, ruleBot, ruleBotDisplayName}: AgentRulesSubtitleProps) {
    const styles = useThemeStyles();
    const {translate} = useLocalize();
    return (
        <View style={[styles.mt2, styles.gap2]}>
            <Text style={[styles.textNormal, styles.colorMuted]}>{translate('workspace.rules.agentRules.subtitle')}</Text>
            {!!ruleBotAccountID && (
                <View style={[styles.flexRow, styles.alignItemsCenter, styles.gap2]}>
                    <Text style={[styles.textNormal, styles.colorMuted]}>{translate('workspace.rules.agentRules.enforcedBy')}</Text>
                    <UserPill accountID={ruleBotAccountID} avatar={ruleBot?.avatar} displayName={ruleBotDisplayName} email={ruleBot?.login} />
                </View>
            )}
        </View>
    );
}
```

This keeps the section's render tree visible and makes the subtitle independently testable and memoizable by React Compiler.

The `AuthScreensInitHandler` component already aggregates multiple unrelated responsibilities (session management, Pusher initialization, navigation setup, app loading). This new `useEffect` for reconciling the high-contrast theme intent after sign-in is an unrelated concern that further adds to the mix. If the high-contrast reconciliation logic changes, it requires navigating through all the other unrelated effects in this component.

Extract this effect into a focused custom hook, for example:

```tsx
function useReconcileHighContrastIntent() {
    const [preferredTheme] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.PREFERRED_THEME);
    const [highContrastIntent] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.SIGN_IN_HIGH_CONTRAST_INTENT);
    const [isLoadingApp] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.IS_LOADING_APP);
    const [session] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.SESSION);
    const wasLoadingApp = useRef<boolean | undefined>(undefined);

    useEffect(() => {
        const hasFinishedLoading = !!wasLoadingApp.current && !isLoadingApp;
        wasLoadingApp.current = isLoadingApp;
        if (!hasFinishedLoading || !highContrastIntent || !session?.authToken) {
            return;
        }
        const currentTheme = preferredTheme ?? CONST.THEME.DEFAULT;
        const targetTheme = getContrastTheme(getBaseTheme(currentTheme));
        if (currentTheme !== targetTheme) {
            User.updateTheme(targetTheme, false);
        }
        Onyx.set(ONYXKEYS.SIGN_IN_HIGH_CONTRAST_INTENT, null);
    }, [isLoadingApp, highContrastIntent, preferredTheme, session?.authToken]);
}
```

Then call `useReconcileHighContrastIntent()` inside `AuthScreensInitHandler` (or in a separate component if appropriate), keeping the concern isolated and independently testable.

`renderComposerSwipeable` and `renderComposer` (line 101) are internal render helper functions defined inside the component body. They close over the entire component scope, preventing React Compiler from independently memoizing them, and they hide the actual render tree from the component's return statements.

Extract these into standalone components with explicit props:

```tsx
function ComposerSwipeable({reportID, isEditOnly}: {reportID: string; isEditOnly?: boolean}) {
    return (
        <SwipeableView onSwipeDown={Keyboard.dismiss}>
            <ReportActionCompose reportID={reportID} isEditOnly={isEditOnly} />
        </SwipeableView>
    );
}
```

Then use `<ComposerSwipeable reportID={reportIDFromRoute} />` and `<ComposerSwipeable reportID={reportIDFromRoute} isEditOnly />` directly in the JSX return statements.

`renderReportActionItemFragments` is an internal render helper defined inside the component body and called in the return statement (`renderReportActionItemFragments(isApprovedOrSubmittedReportAction)`). This closure captures the entire component scope, preventing React Compiler from independently memoizing it.

Extract this into a separate component with explicit props:
```tsx
function ReportActionItemFragments({fragments, action, reportID, iouMessage, style, displayAsGroup, isApprovedOrSubmittedReportAction, isHoldReportAction, shouldWrapInText}: Props) {
    const styles = useThemeStyles();
    const reportActionItemFragments = fragments.map((fragment, index) => (
        <ReportActionItemFragment
            key={`actionFragment-${action.reportActionID}-${index}`}
            // ... props
        />
    ));
    return shouldWrapInText ? <Text style={styles.ltr}>{reportActionItemFragments}</Text> : <>{reportActionItemFragments}</>;
}
```
Then use it in the return:
```tsx
{!isHidden ? (
    <ReportActionItemFragments ... shouldWrapInText={isApprovedOrSubmittedReportAction} />
) : (
    <Text ...>{translate('moderation.flaggedContent')}</Text>
)}
```

`renderButton` is an internal render helper function that returns JSX and is called as `{renderButton()}` in the component's return statement. Internal render helpers close over the entire component scope, preventing the React Compiler from independently memoizing them, and they hide the component's render tree.

Extract `renderButton` into its own component with explicit props, or inline the conditional rendering directly in the JSX return:

```tsx
// Option 1: Extract to a separate component
function ActionButtonContent({reportPreviewAction, ...props}: ActionButtonContentProps) {
    if (reportPreviewAction === CONST.REPORT.REPORT_PREVIEW_ACTIONS.SUBMIT) {
        return <SubmitActionButton ... />;
    }
    // ... other cases
}

// Option 2: Inline the conditionals directly in JSX
return (
    <View style={[buttonMaxWidth, styles.flex1, {height: variables.h40}]}>
        {reportPreviewAction === CONST.REPORT.REPORT_PREVIEW_ACTIONS.SUBMIT && <SubmitActionButton ... />}
        {reportPreviewAction === CONST.REPORT.REPORT_PREVIEW_ACTIONS.APPROVE && <ApproveActionButton ... />}
        {/* ... */}
    </View>
);
```

