The Header component now mixes its primary rendering responsibility with dialog label registration logic across three separate `useEffect` hooks (register/update label, mark ready, cleanup). Per the rule's guidance to "group effects that serve the same purpose into one hook," these three effects should be extracted into a focused custom hook.

Extract the dialog label lifecycle management into a dedicated hook, for example:

```tsx
// useDialogLabelRegistration.ts
function useDialogLabelRegistration(title: string | ReactNode, screenWrapperStatus: ScreenWrapperStatusContextValue | null) {
    const {isInsideDialog, pushLabel, popLabel, updateLabel, markReady} = useDialogLabelActions();
    const labelIdRef = useRef<number | undefined>(undefined);

    useEffect(() => {
        if (!isInsideDialog || typeof title !== 'string' || !title) {
            return;
        }
        if (labelIdRef.current === undefined) {
            labelIdRef.current = pushLabel(title);
        } else {
            updateLabel(labelIdRef.current, title);
        }
    }, [isInsideDialog, title, pushLabel, updateLabel]);

    useEffect(() => {
        if (!isInsideDialog || labelIdRef.current === undefined || !screenWrapperStatus?.didScreenTransitionEnd) {
            return;
        }
        markReady(labelIdRef.current);
    }, [isInsideDialog, screenWrapperStatus?.didScreenTransitionEnd, markReady]);

    useEffect(
        () => () => {
            if (labelIdRef.current === undefined) {
                return;
            }
            popLabel(labelIdRef.current);
            labelIdRef.current = undefined;
        },
        [popLabel],
    );

    return isInsideDialog;
}
```

Then in Header.tsx, replace the three effects with a single hook call:
```tsx
const isInsideDialog = useDialogLabelRegistration(title, screenWrapperStatus);
```

This gives the logic a clear name, isolates it for independent testing, and keeps Header focused on rendering.

