`SplashScreenStateContextProvider` was a focused context provider that managed only splash screen state. This PR adds a `useEffect` that orchestrates deferred module loading -- an unrelated responsibility. Module initialization (Onyx subscriptions, push notification handlers, pagination config) is a distinct concern from splash screen state management. Coupling them means that changes to the deferred loading strategy require modifying the splash screen context, and vice versa.

Extract the deferred module loading into a dedicated hook (e.g., `usePostSplashModuleLoader`) and call it from `Expensify.tsx` or a dedicated initialization component where other startup orchestration already lives:

```tsx
// usePostSplashModuleLoader.ts
function usePostSplashModuleLoader() {
    const {splashScreenState} = useSplashScreenState();

    useEffect(() => {
        if (splashScreenState !== CONST.BOOT_SPLASH_STATE.HIDDEN) {
            return;
        }
        loadPostSplashScreenModules();
    }, [splashScreenState]);
}
```

Then consume it from a component that already owns startup orchestration, keeping `SplashScreenStateContextProvider` focused on its single responsibility.

